    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for this. looks good. could you add a little bit more detail to the part about wrist pain? maybe some specific stretches for the hands/wrists so people have something actionable to do when they feel that discomfort setting in?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Subject: Enhancing Your Workstation Ergonomics for Healthier Workdays\n\nDear Team,\n\nI hope this message finds you well. As we continue to navigate the challenges of remote work, it's essential to ensure our workspaces are conducive to both productivity and well-being. Recently, I've noticed a few of you experiencing wrist pain and back aches, which can be mitigated with some simple ergonomic adjustments.\n\n1. Chair Height: Adjust your chair so that your feet rest flat on the floor, with your knees at a 90-degree angle. This position helps maintain proper posture and reduces strain on your back.\n\n2. Monitor Position: Ensure your monitor is at eye level, about an arm's length away. This prevents neck strain and promotes a comfortable viewing angle.\n\n3. Keyboard and Mouse Placement: Keep your keyboard and mouse close to your body, with your wrists in a neutral position. Consider using an ergonomic keyboard and mouse to further reduce strain. If you feel discomfort in your wrists, try these simple stretches:\n\n   a. Wrist Flexor Stretch: Extend your arm in front of you with your palm facing up. Use your other hand to gently pull back on your fingers, stretching the underside of your forearm. Hold for 15-30 seconds",
      "source": "target",
